          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma originates from the posterolateral nasal wa</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma originates from the posterolateral nasal wall near which anatomical landmark?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hiatus semilunaris</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The hiatus semilunaris is the drainage pathway of the maxillary sinus on the lateral nasal wall. JNA arises more posteriorly, near the sphenopalatine foramen, from where it spreads via the pterygopalatine fossa.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sphenopalatine foramen</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>JNA originates close to the sphenopalatine foramen on the posterolateral nasal wall. The adjacent pterygopalatine fossa serves as the anatomical crossroads, enabling spread into the infratemporal fossa, orbit, and middle cranial fossa.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Frontonasal duct</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The frontonasal duct drains the frontal sinus and is located superiorly. JNA arises from the posterolateral nasal wall near the sphenopalatine foramen — a posterior structure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inferior meatus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The inferior meatus is where the nasolacrimal duct opens, located anteroinferiorly. JNA arises posteriorly near the sphenopalatine foramen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "JNA arises near the sphenopalatine foramen on the posterolateral nasal wall. The pterygopalatine fossa adjacent to it is the anatomical crossroads for tumour spread into the orbit, infratemporal fossa, and middle cranial fossa."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The sphenopalatine foramen is the key landmark, and the pterygopalatine fossa is the crossroads through which JNA extends to adjacent regions."
                }
              ],
