          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An adult presents with unilateral serous otitis media, nasal obstruction, and a firm cervical lymph node. Which diagnosis must be excluded before attributing the glue ear to a benign cause?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inverted papilloma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inverted papilloma is a benign nasal tumour with malignant potential, but it does not classically present with serous otitis media and cervical lymphadenopathy. This triad points to nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>JNA occurs in adolescent males and presents with epistaxis and nasal obstruction. It does not typically cause serous otitis media with cervical lymphadenopathy in an adult.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The classic triad of nasopharyngeal carcinoma is unilateral nasal obstruction, serous otitis media from Eustachian tube blockage, and cervical lymphadenopathy. This is EBV-linked, and unilateral glue ear in an adult always requires nasopharyngoscopy to exclude NPC.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maxillary sinus squamous cell carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Maxillary SCC presents with facial swelling, loose upper teeth, and infraorbital numbness due to local invasion. Serous otitis media with cervical lymphadenopathy is the hallmark triad of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This clinical triad — unilateral nasal obstruction, serous otitis media, and cervical lymphadenopathy — is the hallmark of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Remember the anchoring rule: unilateral glue ear in an adult needs nasopharyngoscopy to exclude NPC."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well analysed. Unilateral glue ear in an adult is a red flag for nasopharyngeal carcinoma and always warrants nasopharyngoscopy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
