          "label": "<p>A 16-year-old boy presents with progressive right-sided nasal obstruction and re</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 16-year-old boy presents with progressive right-sided nasal obstruction and recurrent severe nosebleeds. Examination reveals a fleshy mass in the nasopharynx. What should be the immediate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Incisional biopsy under local anaesthesia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Biopsy is absolutely contraindicated in suspected JNA. The tumour's vessels lack a muscular wall and cannot vasospasm, so biopsy can trigger life-threatening arterial haemorrhage from the internal maxillary artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Course of empirical antibiotics</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Empirical antibiotics are inappropriate when clinical features point to JNA. An adolescent male with severe epistaxis and a nasopharyngeal mass needs imaging, not a trial of antibiotics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contrast CT of the paranasal sinuses</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In suspected JNA, contrast CT is the first investigation. It reveals a brightly enhancing mass and the pathognomonic Holman-Miller sign — anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary wall — confirming the diagnosis without the haemorrhage risk of biopsy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate surgical excision without embolisation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Operating without pre-operative embolisation risks catastrophic intraoperative bleeding. The two-step protocol requires embolisation of the internal maxillary artery 24 to 72 hours before surgical excision.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For suspected JNA in an adolescent male, the clinical algorithm is: order contrast CT first (look for the Holman-Miller sign), then MRI for extension mapping, followed by pre-operative embolisation and surgical excision. Never biopsy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well applied. In suspected JNA, contrast CT comes first — it confirms the diagnosis with the Holman-Miller sign and avoids the catastrophic bleeding risk of biopsy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
